<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charles Grant, 1st Baron Glenelg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1778">1778</a>–<a href="/years/1866">1866</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Lord Glenelg</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charles Grant, 1st Baron Glenelg, was a Scottish politician who served as Secretary of State for War and the Colonies from 1835 to 1839. Influenced by humanitarian critics of colonial violence, he ordered the abandonment of the Province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adelaide-of-saxe-meiningen">Queen Adelaide</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> in 1836. He was criticised for his handling of the Canadian rebellions of 1837 and left office in 1839.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-grant-1st-baron-glenelg.jpg" alt="Charles Grant, 1st Baron Glenelg" width="320" height="385" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Henry Perronet Briggs/Art UK · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Henry_Perronet_Briggs_-_Charles_Grant,_Baron_Glenelg_(1837).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
